<section class="section" id="process">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="task-section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">From sample to batch</p><h2>Check the details while they are easy to change.</h2><p>A full enclosure is not always the first useful print. A small section can test a connector opening, mounting point or joint before committing to a larger body.</p></div>
    <ol class="task-process">
      <li><h3>Define the job</h3><p>Share the equipment, available files, use conditions and quantity. We establish what needs designing and what is already resolved.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Review the design</h3><p>Agree mounting, clearances, fixings and material. For CAD work, drawings or rendered views help confirm the proposed geometry.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Test the fit</h3><p>Use a trial piece or prototype to assess the important features. For a batch, review a sample in the proposed material before approval.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Make and assemble</h3><p>Produce the agreed parts, with finishing and assembly where specified. Required checks and delivery arrangements form part of the quote.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="task-batch-note"><h3>Have a repeat production requirement?</h3><p>Tell us about the first order and expected reorders. Once the design and sample are agreed, manufacture can focus on the defined part specification. <a href="{{ '/services/batch-production/' | url }}">See batch &amp; commercial orders</a>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-muted">
  <div class="container task-brief-grid">
    <div class="task-section-copy"><p class="eyebrow">A useful project brief</p><h2>Tell us how it needs to fit and work.</h2><p>You do not need to choose a plastic before getting in touch. We discuss the use conditions and practical trade-offs before recommending a material.</p><p>Prototype work can also identify when printing is unsuitable. Our <a href="{{ '/case-studies/chocolate-decorating-tool-prototypes/' | url }}">three-version decorating-tool study</a> ended with a decision against production because the risk of small ribs breaking remained unresolved.</p></div>
    <ul class="task-brief-list"><li><strong>Equipment and space</strong>Device models, available dimensions and any drawings or files.</li><li><strong>Mounting and access</strong>Fixing points, connectors, cable clearance and how the assembly opens.</li><li><strong>Use conditions</strong>Load, temperature, sunlight, moisture, chemicals and appearance.</li><li><strong>Quantity and scope</strong>A prototype, a batch or repeat supply; any finishing, hardware or firmware required.</li></ul>
  </div>
</section>
